`$fast_math`, `$safe_math` and `$infallible` are compiler-owned numeric
constraints. They refine how a function or complete statement computes and
which numeric guarantees it exposes. Ordinary checked arithmetic stays the
default, so a program needs no constraint to get the existing checked
behaviour.

Each constraint adds one guarantee. It does not make every operation succeed,
make a call faster or widen target support. The Progress matrix owns
implementation status: @../progress (Progress).

### Placement and reach

The three names are parameterless source modifiers. Write `$fast_math`, never
`$fast_math()`. They follow the ordinary source-directive attachment rules and
may share a line with whitespace in any order:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $fast_math $infallible
    increment |value I32| -> I32 {
        return value + 1
    }

    $safe_math
    recover |left I32, right I32| -> I32 {
        value = left + right catch then 0
        return value
    }
]

A blank physical line, a scope boundary or the end of the file breaks
attachment. Repeating a name on one target is an error that identifies both
occurrences, including repetition without arguments.

Valid targets are functions, methods, generic functions and complete executable
statements. A compound statement includes its condition, branches and body.
A function annotation covers the function body. A runtime root statement is a
valid target.

Imports, parameters, fields, types, export groups, compile-time-only
declarations and template heads are not targets. Compile-time function bodies
are not constraint targets either, so `$fast_math`, `$safe_math` and
`$infallible` on any statement inside a `#` function body, at any depth, are
rejected, as they are on a `#` function header. Moth has no file-level numeric
mode.

An annotation applies to the lexical scope of its target. It covers
caller-authored operands, binding initialisers, right-hand expressions and
compound write-backs in that scope. A callee body keeps its own contract, and a
call never propagates the caller's mode into the callee. Explicit `cast`,
comparisons, logical operations, assertions, collection operations, memory
checks and foreign validation keep their current contracts.

### `$fast_math`

`$fast_math` selects fast semantics only for resolved fixed-integer and
binary-float arithmetic. It does not make unrelated calls, `cast` or `Dec`
operations fast. A fast numeric failure traps rather than taking an implicit
recoverable `Error` edge. In a required-const context, a fast operation that
cannot fold is a compile-time diagnostic instead of a trap. Fast mode never
weakens storage or bounds invariants.

A selected fast operation counts as fast use even when it later folds to a
constant or its result becomes dead. A fast annotation with no reachable fast
operation is vacuous. The exact operator rules, compound write-back rules,
range-loop traps and backend parity contract live in
@../../developer-docs/numeric-backend-contract (Numeric backend contract).

### `$safe_math`

`$safe_math` guarantees that no executed or statically selected reachable
computation uses fast numeric semantics. The guarantee follows direct, generic,
recursive and module calls, and any trusted provider contract that exposes fast
behaviour. A fast range guard counts as fast use even when the loop header
contains no authored arithmetic. This includes a guard in a statically skipped
handler or in statements after a terminator.

A generic function's constraints are verified separately for each materialised
instance. A dormant generic template has no executable body, so it publishes no
guarantee until an instance is materialised.

An unclassified external call rejects `$safe_math` because its fast behaviour
cannot be certified absent. Replace the callable or use an officially trusted
implementation whose closed numeric contract proves the required absence.

`$safe_math` does not guarantee that arithmetic succeeds. An ordinary checked
operation can still fail, and a boundary can still return an error or trap for
an unrelated reason. A value that previously wrapped is an ordinary valid value
with no history taint.

### `$infallible`

`$infallible` guarantees that no recoverable error escapes its boundary and no
reachable Moth semantic panic or trap can execute. It admits proven-total
operations and locally recovered errors only when the complete recovery path
satisfies the guarantee. It excludes environmental exhaustion, external
termination and implementation faults, and it does not prove termination.

A statement boundary ends at statement completion or escape. A function
boundary ends at its return. A missing error slot does not make a function
infallible. An `Error!` function can satisfy `$infallible` while keeping its
declared return contract. The slot stays in the ABI and keeps catch
eligibility even when no failure escapes.

A failure caught inside the statement is contained, but the handler's own traps
and escaping errors remain part of the boundary. A trap or error outside the
annotated statement does not reject it. A boundary in a handler that no live
failure edge reaches is not checked, while fast selection inside a selected
handler still counts for the enclosing statement.

Authored `catch` eligibility stays separate from actual failure production.
The existing expression-level call-count and nesting rules still decide where a
catch is legal, and a currently unreachable handler still receives ordinary
source validation. An unproved assertion, fast trap or escaping error rejects
the annotation.

### Combining constraints

Names may share a target and their order does not change the result.
`$safe_math $fast_math` first selects the lexical fast mode, then rejects the
boundary only when an actual relevant fast operation or contract is reachable.
A Dec-only computation remains valid when its transitive calls also meet the
safe guarantee, because Dec never enters fast mode. `$fast_math $infallible`
is valid when the fast computation is proved trap-free.

A fast range guard is a fast operation for this check. Combining `$fast_math`
with `$safe_math` rejects a fast range even when the loop body contains no
authored arithmetic.

### Proof policy

The proof policy is bounded and target-independent. It uses exact type bounds,
valid constants, existing mandatory failure discharge and narrow local facts.
Accepted local facts are a nonzero literal or folded divisor, a signed divisor
whose literal or folded value is not `-1` and a non-negative literal or folded
exponent. A fast operation with no trap cause counts as total. Nothing else
proves an operation safe. An unproved potential error or semantic trap fails
`$infallible`. It never runs SMT, general loop induction or whole-program path
solving.

A reachable assertion, unknown external call, caller-side validation of an
external float result, a store conversion that can trap, an unproved fast trap
or an escaping recoverable edge rejects `$infallible`. The check follows direct,
generic, recursive and module calls through the existing monotone convergence.

Semantic reachability is checked after profile-independent static selection,
before any optional optimisation can erase evidence. A selected fast operation
counts even when an allowed deterministic Stage 4 pure fold or later dead-result
elimination removes it. Required compile-time execution may diagnose statically
invalid arithmetic before runtime HIR exists. This does not weaken `$infallible`:
the compiled runtime has no escaping error or semantic trap from an operation
successfully evaluated at compile time. `$safe_math` remains a guarantee about
selected arithmetic semantics, not merely arithmetic instructions remaining
after folding.

Inactive ordinary branches still receive their required syntax and type
validation. Mandatory catch, assertion and authored fast-use validation survives
semantic selection as specified by its owning rules. Optional folding of ordinary
runtime functions cannot contribute to static selection, change constraints or
remove call provenance before verification. With identical semantic inputs,
debug and release must receive equivalent constraint verdicts and validated HIR.

Private and exported annotations are checked at their declaration, even when
no selected caller reaches the function. Facades, public interfaces and
re-exports preserve the guarantee.

### External calls and trusted providers

Moth-source packages are analysed normally. User-authored Rust host, browser
JavaScript, WAT and WIT/component bindings cannot establish the absence of fast
operations, traps or escaping failures from a type signature or package claim.
Only compiler-maintained packages and approved vendored Rust libraries
may have trusted evidence, issued through a compiler-controlled registration.
Ordinary builder SDK and source registrations cannot grant that authority.

Trust belongs to a known implementation, while the closed numeric contract
belongs to each callable. An approved implementation must declare which
behaviours are possible rather than receiving `$safe_math` or `$infallible`
automatically. A trusted callable with known fast behaviour fails `$safe_math`.
A trusted callable with possible traps or escaping errors fails `$infallible`.
An unknown callable fails both guarantees when reachable, including through a
Moth-source wrapper. Unused foreign imports do not taint unrelated functions.
The compiler propagates the evidence through ordinary call summaries, facades
and public interfaces before target optimisation can discard it.

This evidence concerns Moth semantic traps and recoverable errors, including
required compiler-generated foreign value guards. Native host panics,
operational adapter faults and external termination remain separate runtime
faults outside the `$infallible` proof. Approval of a Rust implementation
requires separate review of those risks rather than treating the directive as
a promise that arbitrary Rust execution cannot panic.

Declared guarantees, known positive hazards, proved absence and provisional or
unknown summaries stay distinct. An unknown summary cannot prove absence for
`$infallible`.

### Trap boundary

A fast numeric failure is a semantic trap. `catch` handles recoverable `Error`
only, so it never intercepts a trap. Work done before a trap is not undone, so
side effects such as `io.line` output remain. If a trap happens before a
compound store commits, the target keeps its previous value. The trapping
invocation publishes none of its runtime fragments.

A trap ends the current guest call and invalidates the execution instance
selected by the builder's lifecycle. That instance cannot run later Moth code.
The host may construct a replacement instance. The quarantine contract and
the JavaScript and Wasm transport rules, including the rule that no reset
helper exists, belong to
@../../developer-docs/numeric-backend-contract (Numeric backend contract)
and `crates/lappet/docs/host-abi.md` > `Faults`.

### Examples

These are the minimum valid examples:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $safe_math $infallible
    sum_small |left I8, right U8| -> I16 {
        return left + right
    }

    $fast_math $infallible
    wrap_small |left U8, right U8| -> U8 {
        return left + right
    }

    $fast_math $safe_math
    dec_only |left Dec4, right Dec4| -> Dec4 {
        return left + right
    }
]

The first function is valid because every `I8 + U8` sum fits `I16`. Equal
narrow types do not widen, so `I8 + I8` and `U8 + U8` may overflow in ordinary
mode. The second function is infallible because wrapping addition cannot trap.
The third is safe because Dec uses ordinary semantics.

These combinations are invalid:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- INVALID: reaches wrap_small's fast arithmetic
    $safe_math
    call_wrapper |left U8, right U8| -> U8 {
        return wrap_small(left, right)
    }

    -- INVALID: the divisor is not proved nonzero
    $fast_math $infallible
    divide |left I32, right I32| -> I32 {
        return left // right
    }

    -- INVALID: the handler's checked arithmetic can overflow
    $infallible
    halve_or_step |left I32, right I32| -> I32 {
        value I32 = left // right catch then left - 1
        return value
    }
]

The division failure is caught, but the handler's checked `left - 1` can
overflow when `left` is `I32` minimum. That failure escapes the `$infallible`
boundary.

### Deferred Channels constraint

The accepted Channels V1 design adds `$no_suspend` as an optional function,
method and generic-function guarantee. It requires proof that reachable
execution, including implicit child joins and cancellation cleanup, cannot
suspend. It does not grant suspension permission, change ordinary call syntax
or imply `$infallible`. Use canonical inferred suspension summaries and trusted
provider evidence, including recursion and concrete materialisations. Unknown
external effects cannot prove absence. Ordinary runtime functions remain
unannotated. See @../async (Channels). This constraint is not implemented.
